WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.372
Billions of tiny devices sense the world around us.

2
00:00:03.372 --> 00:00:07.751
Could they learn together, without a server and without sharing raw data?

3
00:00:07.751 --> 00:00:11.207
Decentralized federated learning promises that.

4
00:00:11.207 --> 00:00:14.515
But with kilobytes of memory and slow radios,

5
00:00:14.515 --> 00:00:18.538
swapping full models every round is barely feasible.

6
00:00:18.538 --> 00:00:25.065
And existing fixes split models at random, as if every parameter mattered equally.

7
00:00:25.065 --> 00:00:30.859
Our answer is tinyGist: each device shares just the gist of its model.

8
00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:34.622
First, it groups parameters into segments by magnitude,

9
00:00:34.622 --> 00:00:37.628
which is a nearly free importance signal.

10
00:00:37.628 --> 00:00:43.228
Second, important segments are shared more often, and the rest now and then,

11
00:00:43.228 --> 00:00:45.123
keeping models diverse.

12
00:00:45.123 --> 00:00:51.691
Third, it weights each update by the sender's accuracy, and adapts the learning rate.

13
00:00:51.691 --> 00:00:56.987
We built it end to end: in simulation, in emulation with hundreds of devices,

14
00:00:56.987 --> 00:00:59.932
and on real microcontroller clusters.

15
00:00:59.932 --> 00:01:06.592
Across eight models and fifteen tasks, tinyGist converges up to 3.3 times faster,

16
00:01:06.592 --> 00:01:12.055
gains up to 17% accuracy, and sends about three times less data.

17
00:01:12.055 --> 00:01:16.857
And it stays ahead, in simulation and on real devices.

18
00:01:16.857 --> 00:01:22.796
We also validate our importance proxy, and analyze differential privacy and secure aggregation.

19
00:01:22.796 --> 00:01:28.603
I'm Navidreza Asadi and I invite you to read the paper and try tinyGist yourself.

